  "question": {
    "text": "A monochromatic plane wave of speed c and wavelength <math> <mrow> <mi>λ</mi> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;is diffracted at a small aperture. The diagram illustrates successive wavefronts (vibrating in the same phase). After what time will some portion of the wavefront <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>XY</mi></math> reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>?<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0089_32780aa7cc4d.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 117px; height: 148px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mi>&lambda;</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mi>&lambda;</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn> <mi>λ</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>4</mn> <mi>&lambda;</mi> </mrow> <mrow> <mtext>c</mtext> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "This distance between the 2 successive lines (wavefronts) is <math> <mi>λ</mi> </math>. This is for both the straight and curved lines.<br /><br />So, wavefront XY is separated by a distance of <math> <mrow> <mn>3</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow> </math> from the wavefront that has reached point P.<br /><br />Distance that needs to be traveled<math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow> </math><br /><br />Speed of light <math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow> </math><br /><br />Speed = Distance/Time<br /><br />Time = distance/speed <math> <mrow> <mo>=</mo><mfrac> <mrow> <mn>3</mn><mi>λ</mi></mrow> <mi>c</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>"
  }
